      {
        "id": "post-rpm-lad-effective-equation",
        "kind": "postulate",
        "domain": "physics",
        "title": "Flat Lorentz–Abraham–Dirac effective equation",
        "statement": "Restrict [[def-rpm-charge-spin-model]] to its flat affine Minkowski branch, with signature $(-,+,+,+)$, proper time $\\tau$ in seconds, future four-velocity $U=dx/d\\tau$ satisfying $\\eta(U,U)=-c^2$, constant primitive $m>0$ in kilograms and nonzero charge $q\\in\\mathbb R$ in coulombs. Let the prescribed external electromagnetic two-form $F$ be $C^2$ on an open neighborhood of the worldline; use the component convention $F_{0i}=-E_i/c$, $F_{ij}=\\epsilon_{ijk}B_k$ of [[def-em-electromagnetic-tensor]]. Let $a:=dU/d\\tau$ and\n\n$$\\alpha:=q/m,\\qquad \\tau_e:=\\frac{q^2}{6\\pi\\epsilon_0mc^3}>0,\\qquad f_0^a:=\\alpha F^a{}_bU^b,$$\n\nwhere $\\epsilon_0=(\\mu_0c^2)^{-1}$ follows [[def-em-si-conventions]]. Adopt the flat LAD effective equation\n\n$$a^a=f_0^a+\\tau_e\\left(\\frac{da^a}{d\\tau}-\\frac{\\eta_{bc}a^ba^c}{c^2}U^a\\right).$$\n\nThe derivative is the ordinary derivative in an inertial affine chart. Its dimensions are consistent: $a$ and $f_0$ are accelerations, the parenthesized term has acceleration per second, and $\\tau_e$ is a time. This is an adopted effective model equation for sufficiently regular curves (at least $C^3$); it is not asserted to be an exact law of nature, a point-source Maxwell construction, or a globally well-posed dynamics. In particular, no existence, uniqueness, stability, or empirical claim is part of this postulate.\n\nUse of the equation as a reduced physical approximation carries the source qualification that radiation reaction be small relative to the applied Lorentz force in the particle's instantaneous rest frame (the inertial frame momentarily comoving with $U$). This postulate neither asserts that condition for arbitrary $F$ nor supplies a numerical threshold; the qualification is not used to deduce the local ODE results or the force-free counterexample.",
        "deps": [
          "def-rpm-charge-spin-model",
          "def-em-electromagnetic-tensor",
          "def-em-si-conventions"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-rpm-charge-spin-model": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "def-em-electromagnetic-tensor": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "def-em-si-conventions": "formulation-prerequisite"
        },
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Landau and Lifshitz, The Classical Theory of Fields, 4th revised English edition",
              "url": "https://ru.djvu.online/file/ADnW1S0k8Ttos",
              "locator": "Complete text inspected; §§75–76, printed pp. 222–229. Gaussian-unit formulas are not copied as SI coefficients."
            },
            {
              "title": "Sen Zhang, Pre-acceleration from Landau–Lifshitz Series, arXiv:1303.7120",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/1303.7120",
              "locator": "Full HTML text inspected; §2, Eqs. (2.1)–(2.6), and Appendix A, Eqs. (A.1)–(A.4), discussing the LAD equation and its reduced-order use."
            }
          ]
        },
        "dependency_level": 2,
        "physical_scope": "Adopted flat-space point-particle effective equation in a prescribed external field. It is a model postulate, not a derivation from a coupled Maxwell point source, finite-body limit, or observation; it asserts no solution existence or global accuracy.",
        "library": "physics",
        "empirical_premises": []
      },
      {
        "id": "rem-rpm-self-force-status",
        "kind": "remark",
        "domain": "physics",
        "title": "Self-force status and point-particle limits",
        "statement": "The present pair prescribes an external field and adopts the flat LAD equation as a separate effective model. It does not construct a coupled Maxwell field sourced by a point charge or evaluate a self-field on the worldline. [[def-rpm-body-moments]] defines moments for a smooth finite body (and a local curved moment convention); its definitions do not assert a point limit or a self-force law.\n\nPoisson, Pound, and Vega's §22.1 records the formal point-charge equations and the singularity of the field on the worldline; §§22.2–22.6 develop the electromagnetic field near the worldline, singular/regular field split, tail, and equation of motion, particularly §22.6. A self-force calculation therefore requires a specified regularization or extended-body limiting argument. Gralla, Harte, and Wald's conclusions are conditional on assuming a one-parameter family $(F_{\\mu\\nu}(\\lambda),J^\\mu(\\lambda),T^M_{\\mu\\nu}(\\lambda))$ of exact solutions to Maxwell's equations, current conservation, and total stress-energy conservation, with the support/scaling hypothesis (i) of their §II and a field decomposition satisfying hypothesis (ii): a retarded self-field plus an external homogeneous Maxwell solution jointly smooth in $\\lambda$ and spacetime. In that scaling limit the body size, charge, and mass tend to zero while the charge-to-mass ratio has a finite limit; the paper does not construct such a family or a constitutive matter model. Under those assumptions, the leading motion is Lorentz-force motion and §IV derives the first-order correction, including self-force, electromagnetic-dipole, and spin terms. In §§V–VI the authors discuss and qualify a self-consistent reduction-of-order formulation; the reduced-order ALD case is tied to negligible spin and electromagnetic-dipole contributions. These conditional results do not establish an unrestricted fixed-charge point construction, arbitrary-duration global accuracy, or the physical truth of the separately adopted postulate [[post-rpm-lad-effective-equation]].",
        "deps": [
          "def-rpm-body-moments",
          "post-rpm-lad-effective-equation"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-rpm-body-moments": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "post-rpm-lad-effective-equation": "formulation-prerequisite"
        },
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Eric Poisson, Adam Pound, and Ian Vega, The Motion of Point Particles in Curved Spacetime, Living Reviews in Relativity 14 (2011)",
              "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.12942/lrr-2011-7",
              "locator": "Relevant Springer article text inspected; §22.1 introduces the point-charge equations and singularity on the worldline; §§22.2–22.6 develop the electromagnetic field near the worldline, singular/regular split, tail, and equation of motion, particularly §22.6."
            },
            {
              "title": "Samuel E. Gralla, Abraham I. Harte, and Robert M. Wald, A Rigorous Derivation of Electromagnetic Self-force, Physical Review D 80, 024031 (2009)",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/0905.2391",
              "locator": "Relevant text inspected; §II, assumptions (i)–(ii), assumes a one-parameter family with the stated support/scaling form and a retarded self-field plus a jointly smooth homogeneous external field, satisfying Maxwell's equations, current conservation, and total-stress-energy conservation; §IV derives the first-order correction; §§V–VI discuss a qualified reduction-of-order formulation. The paper assumes rather than constructs such a family."
            }
          ]
        },
        "dependency_level": 3,
        "physical_scope": "Scope remark separating prescribed-field LL/LAD effective equations, finite-body moments and literature results for a specific shrinking-body family. It supplies no point-charge self-field, universal fixed-charge point limit, or quantitative global accuracy claim.",
        "library": "physics",
        "empirical_premises": [],
        "proof_strategy": "direct"
      }
    ]
