        "id": "ex-rpm-al-future-boundary-preacceleration",
        "kind": "example",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "Future-boundary solution of the nonrelativistic Abraham–Lorentz equation",
        "statement": "Let $m>0$ and $\\tau_e>0$. Let $T>0$ and let $F_{\\rm ext}\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R)$ be nonnegative, not identically zero, and supported in $[0,T]$; $C_c^\\infty$ means smooth with compact support. Here $F_{\\rm ext}$ is a scalar force history in newtons. Consider the separate nonrelativistic Abraham–Lorentz model equation for acceleration $a(t)$,\n\n$$a(t)=\\frac{F_{\\rm ext}(t)}{m}+\\tau_e\\frac{da}{dt}(t),$$\n\nwhere $\\tau_e$ has units of time. A candidate solution is a continuously differentiable map $a:\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R$ that satisfies this equation pointwise for every $t\\in\\mathbb R$. Impose the future boundary condition $\\lim_{t\\to+\\infty}a(t)=0$. Interpret the integral to $+\\infty$ as a finite Riemann integral after a truncation beyond the compact support, with the value independent of the truncation. The unique candidate solution satisfying that boundary condition is\n\n$$a(t)=\\frac{1}{m\\tau_e}\\int_t^{\\infty}e^{(t-s)/\\tau_e}F_{\\rm ext}(s)\\,ds.$$\n\nIt vanishes for $t\\geq T$ and is strictly positive for every $t<0$. Thus this equation with this future boundary condition has pre-acceleration before the pulse support. The example concerns the stated ODE only; it is not a measurement and does not establish the behavior of finite charges.",
        "deps": [
          "thm-ftc-first-part",
          "thm-continuous-implies-integrable",
          "thm-derivative-of-exponential",
          "thm-chain-rule",
          "thm-algebra-of-derivatives",
          "cor-mean-value-theorem",
